---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Cta from "../components/Cta.astro";
import PageHead from "../components/PageHead.astro";
import {
  AUDIENCES,
  CHECKED,
  NOT_FOR_YOU,
  SESSIONBOARD_URL,
  SESSIONIZE_URL,
  STRUCTURAL,
  WHERE_THEY_WIN,
} from "../data/comparison";

/*
 * For the buyer with three tabs open, and for the colleague who will ask "why
 * not just use Sessionize?" in the next meeting.
 *
 * The rule this page is written under: nothing here characterises a competitor's
 * product as bad, and every row they win is on the page. A comparison that a
 * reader can catch being unfair costs more than the deal it was written to win.
 * See `src/data/comparison.ts` for why there is no feature matrix.
 */
---

<Base
  title="Compare"
  description="Podium against Sessionize and Sessionboard: what each is built for, where the incumbents are stronger, and when you should not pick Podium at all."
>
  <PageHead
    eyebrow="Compare"
    title="Three good answers to different questions."
    lede={`Sessionize and Sessionboard are both reasonable buys, and for some conferences they are the right one. Here is the honest shape of the choice — including the parts where they win. Checked against their own pages in ${CHECKED}.`}
  />

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Who each is for</span>
      <h2>Start here, and you may not need the rest of the page.</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        {
          AUDIENCES.map((a) => (
            <div class="card reveal">
              <h3>{a.name}</h3>
              <p><strong>{a.built_for}</strong></p>
              <p>{a.body}</p>
              {a.href && (
                <p>
                  <a href={a.href} rel="nofollow noopener">Their pricing page →</a>
                </p>
              )}
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">The structural difference</span>
      <h2>Everything below is true regardless of what ships next.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        There is no feature matrix on this page. Building one would mean asserting what two
        proprietary products do from the outside, in a table that goes stale the day either of them
        ships a release. These rows are licensing, hosting and packaging — they do not move.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="matrix">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="mine">Podium</th>
              <th scope="col">Sessionize</th>
              <th scope="col">Sessionboard</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              STRUCTURAL.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{r.row}</th>
                  <td class="mine">{r.podium}</td>
                  <td>{r.sessionize}</td>
                  <td>{r.sessionboard}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="aside">
        Pricing and packaging change. Confirm both on their own pages before you decide —
        <a href={SESSIONIZE_URL} rel="nofollow noopener">Sessionize</a> and
        <a href={SESSIONBOARD_URL} rel="nofollow noopener">Sessionboard</a> — rather than taking a
        competitor's word for it. Including ours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Where they win</span>
      <h2>Five things the incumbents do better, today.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        None of these is fixable by writing a better sentence about it, so here they are as
        sentences instead.
      </p>
      <div class="cards">
        {
          WHERE_THEY_WIN.map((w) => (
            <div class="card reveal">
              <h3>{w.title}</h3>
              <p>{w.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Where Podium wins</span>
      <h2>Four differences worth switching for.</h2>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h3>Sponsor sessions are modelled, not bolted on</h3>
          <p>
            A sponsorship package is a countable set of entitlements — two session slots, a logo
            placement — and a sponsor session consumes one. Used against bought, on the same screen
            as the deal, visible to the sponsor too. Most tools in this category model the call for
            proposals and leave the paid half in email.
          </p>
          <h3>Review integrity is enforced, not requested</h3>
          <p>
            A reviewer who submitted, is credited on, or has declared a conflict with a proposal
            cannot score it. The write is refused. There is no "please recuse yourself" convention
            to rely on and no flag for someone to catch afterwards.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>The rules are written down before the code</h3>
          <p>
            What happens to an unused sponsor slot, who may see a reviewer's score, what a deadline
            does when it passes — specified in a normative model, with a check in CI that fails the
            build when the code and the model disagree. You can read what the tool will do before
            you find out.
          </p>
          <h3>No per-speaker pricing, ever</h3>
          <p>
            Nothing costs more because your CFP went well. You pay your own hosting bill, which for
            a conference-sized event is small, and nobody can change the price between editions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="nextstep"><a href="/pricing">The actual bill, itemised →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Be honest with yourself</span>
      <h2>Do not pick Podium if any of these is true.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        We would rather you found this out on this page than in February. If two or more of these
        land, buy something hosted and enjoy your conference.
      </p>
      <ul class="crosses">{NOT_FOR_YOU.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}</ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Cta
    heading="Still here? Go and use it."
    sub="Fifteen minutes in a conference already mid-flight will tell you more than the rest of this page did."
    secondaryHref="/features"
    secondaryLabel="See the screens first"
  />
</Base>
